.topic intro
<b>1. Introduction</b><br>
<br>
<b>What is SealScript Library?</b><br>
  SSL Is a DLX library for SEAL/Bad Seal
 that can add scripting abilities
 to any program using simple methods.
 It's BASIC-like syntax, it's incredible speed and
 it's customizability makes SealScript Library the
 best choice for your SEAL/Bad Seal application
 and except that, you don't have to make your own
 scripting functions ;).<br>
<br>
For a full example see SSDEMO.C included with SealScript.
.end

.topic using
<b>2. Using SealScript</b><br>
<br>
  To use SealScript Library with your programs is
 very simple. Just include sscript.h file with
 your program like this:<br>
<br>
#include "allegro.h"<br>
#include "seal.h"<br>
#include "sscript.h"<br>
/* other includes */<br>
<br>
and define a SealScript object like this:<br>
<br>
p_sscript ss = NULL;<br>
<br>
then when you initialize your application,<br>
initialize this SealScript object like this:<br>
<br>
ss = sscript_init(malloc(sizeof(t_sscript)));<br>
<br>
and in app_begin - app_end add this:<br>
<br>
if (ap_process == AP_DONE) {<br>
  OBJECT(ss)->done(OBJECT(ss);<br>
  free(ss);<br>
};<br>
<br>
  Before doing anything else you must set some
 SealScript code to the object. To do that use
 the set_code function like this:<br>
<br>
ss->set_code(ss, "' SealScript code goes here");<br>
<br>
you can take code from a t_editor box like this:<br>
<br>
ss->set_code(ss, editor->text);<br>
<br>
<br>
  Then when you want to run this code you MUST
compile it first using the compile function:<br>
<br>
ss->compile(ss);<br>
<br>
and then the execute function to run it:<br>
<br>
ss->execute(ss);<br>
<br>
Note: you can use compile function also to
make syntax error checking because compile
return some codes (see <l errors>section 4<le>).<br>
.end

.topic newcmd
<b>3. Defining new commands</b><br>
<br>
  SealScript's standard commands aren't enough. They
 are only the very basics for very simple scripts
 and in most cases you'll need to define your own
 commands. To do that you have to use the
 define_command function like this:<br>
<br>
ss->define_command(ss, command_name, &command_proc);<br>
<br>
 where command_name is a normal valid SEAL string
 (l_text) that defines new command's name and
 command_proc is a function that will be called when
 this command is executed. command_proc must be in
 this "format":<br>
<br>
void command_proc(l_int args, l_text arg[]);<br>
<br>
 where args is the number of given arguments and arg
 is an array that contains all given arguments. If an
 argument is a variable, arg contains the value of
 this variable.<br>
<br>
example:<br>
<br>
#include "allegro.h"<br>
#include "seal.h"<br>
#include "sscript.h"<br>
#include "sfa.h" /* i guess... i don't have SFA so... */<br>
/* other includes */<br>
<br>
p_sscript ss = NULL;<br>
<br>
/* other code */<br>
<br>
void cmd_play(l_int args, l_text arg[])<br>
{<br>
  p_play pp = SFA_init();<br>
  pp->init_file(pp, arg[0]);<br>
  if (pp) {<br>
    pp->play_file(pp);<br>
  }<br>
}<br>
<br>
/* other code */<br>
<br>
void my_app_init()<br>
{<br>
  /* other code */<br>
  ss = sscript_init(malloc(sizeof(t_sscript)));<br>
  ss->define_command(ss, "PLAY", &cmd_play);<br>
  /* other code */<br>
}<br>
<br>
 Of course somewhere you have to use the compile and
execute functions.<br>
.end

.topic errors
<b>4. Error handling</b><br>
<br>
  Many times, SealScript code that is entered by
 your program's user isn't valid and SealScript
 cannot compile it. So before execute a non-compiled
 code (and crash your system) check if there is
 any compilation error. You can check it using two
 ways. First is to check if compile function has
 returned FALSE. If so then there was a compilation
 error. Second way is after compile's call to check
 if ss->error.code is something else than SSE_NOTHING.
 If so then again there was a compilation error. In
 both cases to determine what compilation error
 happened and where you have to check ss->error.code
 (for error code) and ss->error.line (for code line
 where the compilation error found).<br>
<br>
Here is a list with known error codes:<br>
<br>
<b>SSE_SYNTAX_ERROR:</b><br>
   You'll get this if you don't use the normal
  syntax of a command or if you give invalid
  parameters to some commands (f.e. if you type
  OPEN filename FOR COMPRESSING AS file,
  SealScript will return SSE_SYNTAX_ERROR because
  doesn't know the COMPRESSING work).<br>
<br>
<b>SSE_NOT_ENOUGH_ARGS:</b><br>
   You'll get this if you don't specify all
  needed arguments (parameters) to a command.
  The minimum number of needed arguments is
  depending on the command. An example here is
  that if you use the SET command without any
  parameter.<br>
<br>
<b>SSE_LABEL_NOT_FOUND:</b><br>
   You'll get this if you use the GOTO command
  with a label that you haven't specified
  anywhere in your code.<br>
.end

.topic cmds
<b>5. SealScript standard commands</b><br>
<br>
  Before you read SSL's standard commands you have
 to know three things about SealScript's syntax:<br>
<b>First:</b> you can define comments by adding the "'"
 character in before the comment (note: unlike
 BASIC, you can't use REM command and you can't
 add a comment right to a command).<br>
<b>Second:</b> You can define a label by using the ":"
 character at the end of a line with one word.
 That word will be used as a label (f.e.
 LabelName:)<br>
and <b>Third:</b> You can specify more than one commands
 in one line by separating it with the "|" character
 like this:<br>
<br>
Command1 Parameters for command1 | Command2 Pars | ...<br>
<br>
example:<br>
<br>
IF i = 5 | PRINT "i equals to five" | END<br>
<br>
<br>
 And now here is the list with the commands in
creation order (from oldest to newest):<br>
<br>
<br>
<b>SET var, value</b><br>
  Gives to variable VAR the value VALUE<br>
  example: SET nick, "BadSector"<br>
<br>
<b>PRINT [m1[,m2[,m3[,...]]]]</b><br>
  Shows a message box that contains all parameters
 in one line.<br>
  example: PRINT "Time is ", time<br>
<br>
<b>IF v1 what v2</b><br>
  if WHAT is "<>" and v1 equals to v2 then following
 commands until next END will not be executed. If
 WHAT is "=" and v1 doesn't equals to v2 also
 following commands will not be executed.<br>
  example:<br>
    IF nick = "BadSector"<br>
      PRINT "Hello, Bad Sector :)"<br>
    END<br>
<br>
<b>END</b><br>
  used with IF<br>
<br>
<b>ADD x, y  SUB x, y  MUL x, y  DIV x, y</b><br>
  ADD, SUB, MUL and DIV are used for mathematical
 functions.<br>
<br>
<b>OPEN filename FOR method AS file</b><br>
  allocates memory and sets info to the FILE
 variable about filename and method for file
 handling. filename is a string or a string
 variable. method is one of:<br>
   INPUT       for input from text files<br>
   OUTPUT      for output to text files<br>
              this remakes the file.<br>
   APPEND      for output to the end of existing text files (if file
 doesn't exists, it makes it).<br>
  Warning! Every variable that has set up with
  the OPEN command MUST be freed using the
  CLOSE command.<br>
  example: OPEN "readme.txt" FOR INPUT AS file<br>
<br>
<b>WRITE file, ...</b><br>
  writes all other parameters from 2 to the
 FILE (file must be opened with OPEN before or
 system will probably crash).<br>
 example: WRITE file, nick<br>
<br>
<b>READ file, var</b><br>
  Reads a line from the FILE and stores its
 contents to variable VAR.<br>
 example: READ file, nick<br>
<br>
<b>CLOSE file</b><br>
  Frees allocated memory for FILE.<br>
  example: CLOSE file<br>
<br>
<b>ERASE filename</b><br>
  Deletes file FILENAME from the disk.<br>
  example: ERASE "nickinfo.txt"<br>
<b>BEEP</b><br>
  makes a sound from the PC Speaker.<br>
<b>STOP</b><br>
  stops script's execution<br>
  example: if option = 4 | STOP | END<br>
<b>GOTO label</b><br>
  jumps to label LABEL<br>
<b>TIME var</b>
  stores current time to variable VAR in
 HH:MM:SS format.<br>
<b>MULTITASK ON|OFF</b><br>
  enables/disables SEAL/Bad Seal multitasking.
 If multitasking is enabled then when script
 is processing something, you can access
 other programs and windows, else SEAL/Bad
 Seal will not be accessible. Turning off
 multitasking can increase the speed about
 1000% but it's recommended to be used only
 in special cases. By default MULTITASK is
 ON.<br>
<br>
.end

